module symmetry_mirror

// symmetry_mirror.ri — IDIOM: build a symmetric part once and reflect it.
//
//     let twin = union(arm, mirror(arm, plane_yz(0mm)))
//
// ANTI-PATTERN this replaces: authoring both halves by hand, so that every
// later edit has to be applied twice and the two sides silently drift apart.
//
// Verified contracts:
//
//   `mirror` returns a REFLECTED COPY. It does not move, consume or extend the
//   original, so the result must be unioned back in to get a symmetric part.
//   Measured: volume(arm) = volume(mirror(arm, ...)) = 1e-6 m^3 and
//   volume(union(arm, mirror(arm, ...))) = 2e-6 m^3 — a copy, not a pattern
//   that includes its seed.
//
//   Two call forms, and only two:
//     * plane-value (2 args): mirror(g, plane_yz(0mm))     <- prefer this
//     * scalar      (7 args): mirror(g, ox,oy,oz, nx,ny,nz)
//   Any other arity is a compile diagnostic:
//     "mirror() expects 7 arguments (scalar) or 2 (plane-value), got N".
//
//   In the 7-arg form the ORIGIN components must be dimensioned Lengths while
//   the NORMAL components stay bare dimensionless numbers:
//       mirror(g, 0mm, 0mm, 0mm, 1, 0, 0)     // correct
//       mirror(g,   0,   0,   0, 1, 0, 0)     // WRONG — bare-0 origin
//   The wrong form yields "mirror: ox argument expects Length, got Int; pass a
//   dimensioned length such as `5mm`" and then a hard geometry-compile error
//   ("missing or non-Length argument 'ox' for mirror").
//
//   TRAP, re-scoped: the bare-0 7-arg form above is now a HARD `reify check`
//   failure — a compile-layer ArgTypeMismatch (task 5662) on each of ox, oy and
//   oz, exit 1, and no verdict line at all. What survives is narrower: on the
//   decoded-value route `mirror(g, plane_yz(0))` the same three ox/oy/oz Length
//   errors DO reach check's stderr, yet check still exits 0 and still prints
//   "All constraints satisfied." underneath them. So the surviving warning is
//   about the EXIT STATUS and that trailing verdict line, not about the error
//   being absent — read check's stderr, never its exit code alone. That split
//   is measured behaviour here and is deliberate but not permanent; re-measure
//   rather than trusting this line.
//
//   Plane constructors: plane_xy(z), plane_xz(y), plane_yz(x) — each takes
//   EXACTLY ONE offset argument, naming the axis the plane spans (so the
//   argument is the offset along the remaining, normal axis). There is no
//   generic `plane(...)`.
//
//   TRAP: these datum constructors are arity-blind at the type level, so a
//   wrong-arity `plane_yz(0mm, 0mm)` is NOT a compile error — it evaluates to
//   `undef` with no diagnostic at check time, and any geometry built on it
//   silently fails downstream. Same for `point3`/`axis_*`. Count the arguments.

structure def SymmetryMirror {
    param arm_len   : Length = 40mm
    param arm_width : Length = 8mm
    param hub_r     : Length = 10mm

    // One arm, authored once, sitting entirely on the +X side of the YZ plane.
    let arm = translate(
        box(arm_len, arm_width, arm_width),
        arm_len / 2 + hub_r,
        0mm,
        0mm
    )

    // THE IDIOM: reflect across x = 0 and union the copy back in.
    let arm_mirrored = mirror(arm, plane_yz(0mm))
    let arms = union(arm, arm_mirrored)

    let hub  = cylinder_centered(hub_r, arm_width)
    let body = union(arms, hub)

    // The equivalent 7-arg scalar form: dimensioned origin, bare normal.
    let arm_mirrored_scalar = mirror(arm, 0mm, 0mm, 0mm, 1, 0, 0)

    constraint arm_len > arm_width
    constraint arm_width > 1mm
    constraint hub_r > arm_width / 2
}
